<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1154">1154</a>–1233</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Gökböri, Kökböri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1190 to 1233, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayn-al-din-ali-kuchuk">Zayn al-Din Ali Kuchuk</a>. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia-khatun">Rabi'a Khatun</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, and fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187. He was famed for building hospitals, schools and hostels, and for his lavish public celebrations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawlid">Mawlid</a>, the Prophet's birthday.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori.jpg" alt="Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Begtiginids._Muzaffar_al-Din_K%C3%B6kburi._AH_563-630_(AD_1167-1233)._)Arbil_mint._Dated_AH_587_(AD_1191-2),_obverse._Bareheaded_and_draped_bust_left;_name_and_titles_of_Muzaffar_al-Din_K%C3%B6kburi_in_outer_margin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
